Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:03.08,Caption,,0,0,0,,Hello again,\Nhackers.\NBefore we name the
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.08,0:00:05.76,Caption,,0,0,0,,bug,\Npicture a program's memory as a
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.76,0:00:08.55,Caption,,0,0,0,,wall of numbered spaces.\NA value can
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.55,0:00:11.44,Caption,,0,0,0,,live in one space.\NThe number written
Dialogue: 0,0:00:11.44,0:00:14.32,Caption,,0,0,0,,on its door is an address.\NEverything
Dialogue: 0,0:00:14.32,0:00:17.20,Caption,,0,0,0,,else in this story follows from that.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:17.20,0:00:19.33,Caption,,0,0,0,,An address is not the value.\NIt
Dialogue: 0,0:00:19.33,0:00:21.82,Caption,,0,0,0,,is a direction:\Ngo to this location.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.82,0:00:24.49,Caption,,0,0,0,,A pointer is just a variable holding
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.49,0:00:27.43,Caption,,0,0,0,,that direction.\NCopy the pointer,\Nand you
Dialogue: 0,0:00:27.43,0:00:30.80,Caption,,0,0,0,,have copied the direction,\Nnot duplicated the
Dialogue: 0,0:00:30.80,0:00:32.05,Caption,,0,0,0,,thing it reaches.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:32.05,0:00:34.87,Caption,,0,0,0,,On the heap,\Nprograms ask for a
Dialogue: 0,0:00:34.87,0:00:38.39,Caption,,0,0,0,,fresh patch of storage,\Nthen build an
Dialogue: 0,0:00:38.39,0:00:42.61,Caption,,0,0,0,,object inside it.\NThis challenge calls that
Dialogue: 0,0:00:42.61,0:00:46.83,Caption,,0,0,0,,object user.\NIt contains two directions:\None
Dialogue: 0,0:00:46.83,0:00:49.18,Caption,,0,0,0,,to a function,\Nand one to a
Dialogue: 0,0:00:49.18,0:00:49.65,Caption,,0,0,0,,name.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:49.65,0:00:52.06,Caption,,0,0,0,,Some objects have a lifetime:\Nthey are
Dialogue: 0,0:00:52.06,0:00:55.17,Caption,,0,0,0,,created,\Nused,\Nand eventually released.\NFree says
Dialogue: 0,0:00:55.17,0:00:57.97,Caption,,0,0,0,,the program has finished with that storage.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:57.97,0:01:00.62,Caption,,0,0,0,,It does not magically erase every copied
Dialogue: 0,0:01:00.62,0:01:04.04,Caption,,0,0,0,,pointer elsewhere.\NThose directions can outlive the
Dialogue: 0,0:01:04.04,0:01:05.75,Caption,,0,0,0,,object they meant to reach.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:05.75,0:01:08.07,Caption,,0,0,0,,Now we earn the real challenge.\NThe
Dialogue: 0,0:01:08.07,0:01:10.89,Caption,,0,0,0,,local source defines cmd with a function
Dialogue: 0,0:01:10.89,0:01:14.12,Caption,,0,0,0,,pointer named whatToDo followed by a username
Dialogue: 0,0:01:14.12,0:01:17.52,Caption,,0,0,0,,pointer.\NThe program's menu chooses whatToDo,\Nthen
Dialogue: 0,0:01:17.52,0:01:21.17,Caption,,0,0,0,,doProcess calls whichever function address sits in
Dialogue: 0,0:01:21.17,0:01:22.25,Caption,,0,0,0,,the first field.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:22.25,0:01:25.66,Caption,,0,0,0,,That indirect call is ordinary behavior.\NPick
Dialogue: 0,0:01:25.66,0:01:28.44,Caption,,0,0,0,,Subscribe,\Nand the first field holds s.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:28.44,0:01:31.40,Caption,,0,0,0,,doProcess reads that field,\Nthen calls s.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:31.40,0:01:34.36,Caption,,0,0,0,,The surprise is not the instruction.\NThe
Dialogue: 0,0:01:34.36,0:01:37.33,Caption,,0,0,0,,surprise is allowing the same memory to
Dialogue: 0,0:01:37.33,0:01:39.30,Caption,,0,0,0,,later mean something else.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:39.30,0:01:42.53,Caption,,0,0,0,,Delete is the lifetime mistake.\NIt runs
Dialogue: 0,0:01:42.53,0:01:44.75,Caption,,0,0,0,,free on user,\Nbut user is not
Dialogue: 0,0:01:44.75,0:01:47.67,Caption,,0,0,0,,cleared.\NThe main loop keeps the old
Dialogue: 0,0:01:47.67,0:01:51.70,Caption,,0,0,0,,direction.\NThe program has released the storage,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:51.70,0:01:55.24,Caption,,0,0,0,,yet still believes that old address names
Dialogue: 0,0:01:55.24,0:01:57.05,Caption,,0,0,0,,a live command object.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.05,0:01:59.66,Caption,,0,0,0,,Later,\NLeave a message asks for eight
Dialogue: 0,0:01:59.66,0:02:02.54,Caption,,0,0,0,,bytes.\NThe allocator is allowed to reuse
Dialogue: 0,0:02:02.54,0:02:05.67,Caption,,0,0,0,,compatible freed storage.\NIf it returns the
Dialogue: 0,0:02:05.67,0:02:08.11,Caption,,0,0,0,,old user space,\Nthe message and the
Dialogue: 0,0:02:08.11,0:02:11.25,Caption,,0,0,0,,stale user pointer describe the same bytes
Dialogue: 0,0:02:11.25,0:02:13.25,Caption,,0,0,0,,from two different stories.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:13.25,0:02:16.28,Caption,,0,0,0,,Writing the message changes bytes the old
Dialogue: 0,0:02:16.28,0:02:18.97,Caption,,0,0,0,,pointer reads as a command.\NFour bytes
Dialogue: 0,0:02:18.97,0:02:22.35,Caption,,0,0,0,,become the function destination.\NThe next four
Dialogue: 0,0:02:22.35,0:02:25.39,Caption,,0,0,0,,fill the second field.\NWhen doProcess runs
Dialogue: 0,0:02:25.39,0:02:28.08,Caption,,0,0,0,,again,\Nstale data sends control to the
Dialogue: 0,0:02:28.08,0:02:29.55,Caption,,0,0,0,,challenge function.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:29.55,0:02:32.71,Caption,,0,0,0,,That is use-after-free:\Nuse happens after the
Dialogue: 0,0:02:32.71,0:02:36.49,Caption,,0,0,0,,object's lifetime ends.\NThe repair is architectural:
Dialogue: 0,0:02:36.49,0:02:39.39,Caption,,0,0,0,,retire every owner after free,\Nuse clear
Dialogue: 0,0:02:39.39,0:02:43.00,Caption,,0,0,0,,lifetime rules,\Nand validate before indirect use.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:43.00,0:02:46.16,Caption,,0,0,0,,Make addresses and ownership tell the same
Dialogue: 0,0:02:46.16,0:02:46.60,Caption,,0,0,0,,story.
